-- title:
--   Twisted commutant division algebra: uniformiser norm and unit trace
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $L$ be number fields with $L$ an extension of $K$ of degree $2$, let $\sigma$ be a $K$-automorphism of $L$ such that every $K$-automorphism of $L$ lies in the subgroup generated by $\sigma$ (as an integral power), and let $v$ be a nonzero prime of $\mathcal{O}_K$, with completion $K_v$ and its normalised absolute value $\|\cdot\|$. Put $E_v = L \otimes_K K_v$, let $\delta \in \mathrm{GL}_2(E_v)$, and let $D = \mathrm{twistedCommutant}$ be the $K$-subalgebra of $M_2(E_v)$ consisting of those $X$ with $X\delta = \delta\, X^{\sigma}$, where $X^{\sigma}$ is the entrywise image of $X$ under the ring endomorphism $\sigma \otimes \mathrm{id}$ of $E_v$. Assume: a family $e_1,\dots,e_4 \in M_2(E_v)$ is given such that for every $X \in M_2(E_v)$, membership $X \in D$ is equivalent to the existence of a unique $a \in K_v^{4}$ with $X = \sum_i (e_i)\cdot(1 \otimes a_i)$ (entrywise right multiplication by $1 \otimes a_i$); and every nonzero element of $D$ is invertible as a matrix. The conclusion is the conjunction of two assertions: first, there exist $X \in D$ and $n \in K_v$ with $\det X = 1 \otimes n$ and $\|n\| \cdot \#(\mathcal{O}_K/v) = 1$, where $\#(\mathcal{O}_K/v)$ is the absolute norm of the ideal $v$; second, there exist $X \in D$ and $t, n \in K_v$ with $\operatorname{tr} X = 1 \otimes t$, $\det X = 1 \otimes n$, $\|n\| \le 1$ and $\|t\| = 1$.
--
--   In the classical language, the two assertions say that a $\sigma$-twisted commutant which is a quaternion division algebra over $K_v$ and free of rank $4$ contains an element whose reduced norm is a uniformiser (ramification index $2$) and an integral element whose reduced trace is a unit (residue degree $2$). It feeds the local computation of twisted orbital integrals, being used in the determination of the closure condition together with the norm of the determinant and trace of elements of the twisted commutant.
